  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 151 PM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis afternoon an upper-level closed low is over Washington and \nOregon with southerly flow over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This will bring breezy winds to the area with \nthe strongest winds in north-south orientated valleys in \nSouthwestern Montana. There will be isolated showers and \nthunderstorms primarily confined along the Continental Divide this \nafternoon/evening. Some of these showers and thunderstorms could \nmake it as far the I-15 corridor by this evening. It will be around \nseasonal averages today. On Saturday the upper-level closed low \nremains over Washington and Oregon with southerly flow aloft. It \nwill remain warm and breezy on Saturday with showers and \nthunderstorms primarily confined to the continental divide. \n\nOn Sunday the upper-level closed low remains over Washington and \nOregon with southerly flow aloft. As the upper-level low weakens and \nbegins to slide east a shortwave moves through the upper-level \ntrough. This will bring showers and thunderstorms to North-central \nMontana with stronger thunderstorms expected in Blaine and Fergus \nCounty. See the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios Section for \ndetails. On Monday the upper-level trough moves over North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring cooler\ntemperatures and widespread showers and thunderstorms to the area\non Monday. On Tuesday the upper-level trough begins to exit \nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow\ntemperatures to gradually warm up but remain below seasonal \naverages across the area. Rain showers will linger through the day\non Tuesday. On Wednesday and Thursday an upper-level ridge builds\nback in over North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. \nThis will allow temperatures to warm up with mostly dry weather. \n-IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nOn Sunday there is a 30 - 40% chance for thunderstorms along and \neast of a Cut Bank to Great Falls line. For Sunday the Storm \nPrediction Center has issued a marginal risk (5% chance for 58 mph \nwind gusts or greater and/or a 5% chance for hail 1 inch in diameter \nor greater) for severe thunderstorms northeast of a Cut Bank to\nGreat Falls to Judith Gap line. On Monday there is a 25 - 40% \nchance for thunderstorms across North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. \n\nFrom 6 am Sunday through 6 am Tuesday there is greater than a 60% \nchance for 2 inches of rain or greater along the Hi-Line. From 6 am \nSunday through 6 am Tuesday there is a 60 - 80% chance for 1 inch of \nrainfall or greater across North-central Montana. From 6 am Sunday \nthrough 6 am Tuesday there is a 30 - 60% chance for half an inch of \nrain or greater across Central and Southwestern Montana.\nDeterministic models are still flip-floping with respect to where\nthe heaviest precipitation amounts will be. -IG",
